  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely entirely on a desk-based review of policy documents and legal texts rather than field evaluations of enforcement or empirical community impacts.",
      "what": "The study evaluated international, regional, and national policy and legal instruments governing oil exploration within Botswana's Okavango Delta through a qualitative analysis of documentary sources. It found that national laws emphasize licensing and environmental impact assessments to regulate exploration rather than banning it outright in fragile ecosystems, relying on ecosystem-specific frameworks to handle enforcement and public engagement.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that existing legal frameworks in Botswana act to manage and permit oil extraction in critical wetlands rather than prevent it, challenging assumptions that current conservation policies provide strict spatial protection."
    }
